import{_ as i,C as t,o as r,c,ag as o,E as a,j as s,a as h}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"The security model","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"safeguards/security-model.md","filePath":"safeguards/security-model.md"}'),l={name:"safeguards/security-model.md"};function m(u,e,g,p,f,w){const n=t("ControlFigure"),d=t("WalkThrough");return r(),c("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o('<h1 id="the-security-model" tabindex="-1">The security model <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-security-model" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The security model&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The harness gives an agent session several layers of control. Only one of them is a security boundary: the OS sandbox. The other layers prevent accidents. They stop common mistakes and tell the session what to do instead, but a session that tries to get past them can do so. <a href="./risks">Risks of agentic coding</a> says which risk each layer answers.</p><p><code>harness install</code> writes the sandbox, the permission lists and the hooks from the settings template <code>settings/settings.proposal.json</code> into <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>. <a href="./../setup-reference#claude-code">The setup reference</a> gives the steps.</p>',3)),a(n),e[1]||(e[1]=s("p",null,[s("em",null,"The layers of control around one tool call."),h(" The instructions guide the agent. Before a tool call runs, the guard hooks and the permission settings check it, and refuse it, ask you, or let it run. A shell command then runs inside the sandbox.")],-1)),a(d,{name:"tool-call"}),e[2]||(e[2]=o('<p><em>One tool call through the layers of control.</em> The agent reads the instructions and plans a shell command. The guard hooks and the permission settings check the call: a deny refuses it, an ask shows you a prompt, and an allow lets it run. After your yes, the command runs inside the sandbox.</p><h2 id="the-mechanisms" tabindex="-1">The mechanisms <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-mechanisms" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The mechanisms&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each row states the behaviour of the Claude Code version in the last column, in a session with the installed template. A later version can behave differently. A page of this section describes each mechanism: <a href="./sandbox">Sandboxes</a>, <a href="./permissions">Permissions</a>, <a href="./guard-hooks">Guard hooks</a>, <a href="./git-safety">Git safety</a> and <a href="./leaks">Leak checks and gitleaks</a>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">mechanism</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it stops</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it does not stop</th><th style="text-align:left;">Claude Code version</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>The sandbox</strong>, the <code>sandbox</code> section of the template</td><td style="text-align:left;">a shell command that writes outside the write allowlist, that reads a path of the read deny list, or that connects to a host that is not on the network allowlist. The template turns the sandbox on, refuses to start without it, and does not let a command ask to run outside it</td><td style="text-align:left;">a command of <code>excludedCommands</code>, which runs outside the sandbox; the <code>Read</code>, <code>Edit</code> and <code>Write</code> tools, which the permission lists govern; an MCP server, which is not a shell command of the session. The network allowlist names hosts, so it does not stop what a command sends to an allowed host</td><td style="text-align:left;">2.1.293</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>The <code>deny</code> and <code>ask</code> lists</strong>, in <code>permissions</code> of the template</td><td style="text-align:left;">a tool call that matches a <code>deny</code> rule: the call is refused. A tool call that matches an <code>ask</code> rule: in the <code>default</code> mode the user sees a prompt. A <code>Read</code> or <code>Edit</code> deny of a path also goes into the sandbox&#39;s read or write deny list, so it stops a shell command too</td><td style="text-align:left;">a command that a rule does not match as text, such as a command inside a string argument. In the <code>auto</code> mode, an <code>ask</code> goes to the auto-mode classifier, which can let the call run with no prompt</td><td style="text-align:left;">2.1.293</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>The guard hooks</strong>, <code>PreToolUse</code> hooks in <code>hooks/</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">on a shell command: a <code>git add</code> that stages more than the paths it names; a shell write to a file in a git working tree; a command that fails in the sandbox with a misleading error, such as a <code>git</code>, <code>gh</code> or <code>glab</code> write that the sandbox does not exempt; a <code>git init</code> or <code>git clone</code> inside a git working tree; a <code>gh api</code> write that is not a review or a comment in a repository of an own organisation (a prompt), and a <code>glab api</code> DELETE; a <code>gh pr create</code> that names no repository, and one outside the own organisations (a prompt); a recursive <code>rm</code> outside the worktrees and the scratch directory of the research tree; a <code>git archive</code> that writes its file with <code>-o</code> or <code>--output</code>; a lease push that names a default branch or no branch; a forced worktree removal that loses commits or tracked changes, a cherry-pick outside a worktree or a checkout of an own organisation, and a lease push to another organisation (a prompt). On the directory and worktree tools: a move out of the research tree, and the removal of a worktree (a prompt). On a shell command, a web call and a Kaimon call: a token of a known form of GitHub, GitLab or an <code>sk-</code> provider, as it is or percent-decoded, which the token check <code>token_forms</code> refuses to prevent an accident. A hook that exits with status 2 refuses the call</td><td style="text-align:left;">a token that is encoded in another way, split into parts, given without its prefix or read by a script from a file, which passes <code>token_forms</code>: the check is no boundary against a session that sends a secret out. A command shape that the hook does not parse: each check reads the command text with its quotes and ANSI-C quotes, and each check except those of a shell write, of a misleading sandbox error and of a <code>git init</code> or <code>git clone</code> also reads the scripts of <code>sh -c</code>, <code>bash -c</code> and <code>eval</code>, so a program or a subcommand that a variable or a substitution names, a script on standard input, and a shell write or a <code>git init</code> in such a script pass. A write through the <code>Edit</code> and <code>Write</code> tools. Antigravity runs the guard package with <code>--from antigravity</code>, oh-my-pi through an extension with <code>--from omp</code>, and OpenCode through a plugin with <code>--from opencode</code>; the extension and the plugin refuse a call when the guard cannot answer</td><td style="text-align:left;">2.1.293</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>The deny-write install and its drift warning</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template has an <code>Edit</code> deny for each file and directory that <code>harness install</code> installs from this repository into <code>~/.claude/</code>, so a session cannot change an installed agent, skill, rule, hook or instruction file with a tool or a sandboxed shell command. At each Claude Code or Antigravity session start, <code>hooks/install-drift.py</code> warns when the installed files differ from their sources</td><td style="text-align:left;">a command that runs outside the sandbox, such as a <code>git checkout</code> or <code>git restore</code> in <code>~/.claude/</code> when it is a git repository; an edit of the sources in the checkout, which a session can write and which no rule of the template shows to the user; the next <code>harness install --apply</code> installs that edit. The warning blocks nothing. A skill that does not come from this repository, for example one of the tree instructions, has an <code>ask</code> rule, not a deny</td><td style="text-align:left;">2.1.293</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>The leak gate</strong>, the pre-push hook and CI</td><td style="text-align:left;">a push to <code>main</code> of this repository whose files, paths, added lines or commit messages hold a string of the profile&#39;s <code>leak</code> list, a name of the research tree, or a secret or home path that gitleaks finds. CI runs gitleaks on every push</td><td style="text-align:left;">a push with the hook off, for example in a clone where <code>core.hooksPath</code> is not set; a private string that is not in the <code>leak</code> list; the author and committer of a commit, a tag message and <code>git notes</code>. CI has no profile, so it does not search for the private strings</td><td style="text-align:left;">none: the gate does not depend on Claude Code. gitleaks 8.21 or later</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="the-limits" tabindex="-1">The limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A deny rule is a text pattern.</strong> It matches the text of the command, not what the command does. A command inside a string argument escapes it: <code>sh -c &#39;…&#39;</code>, <code>xargs sh -c</code>, <code>find -exec</code>, <code>julia -e &#39;run(…)&#39;</code>, and <code>awk &#39;BEGIN{system(&quot;…&quot;)}&#39;</code>. This class of commands is open, so a longer deny list does not close it. The sandbox contains such a command; the deny list does not.</li><li><strong>The commands that the template excludes from the sandbox run outside it.</strong> The template excludes <code>git</code>, <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code> and <code>ps</code>. A command runs outside the sandbox when every part of it starts with one of these and no part redirects to or from a file. The sandbox does not wrap such a command, so none of the sandbox&#39;s lists apply to it: not the write allowlist, not the network allowlist, and not the write deny list that holds the <code>Edit</code> denies. The permission lists and the hooks still apply. A command with another part, such as a pipe into <code>cat</code> or a <code>cd</code> to another directory, runs inside the sandbox.</li><li><strong>A session can write the Julia depot, which Julia outside the sandbox loads later.</strong> The template allows writes to <code>~/.julia</code>, so a sandboxed command can change a precompiled cache in <code>~/.julia/compiled</code>, and a Julia process that starts outside every sandbox can then load it. This path stays open by decision: Julia needs a writable depot. In <code>~/.cache</code> the template allows writes to <code>~/.cache/pocl</code> only; the <code>srt</code> settings of a frontend add its own cache, such as <code>~/.cache/claude</code> for <code>claude</code> and <code>~/.cache/opencode</code> for <code>opencode</code>.</li><li><strong>Under <code>agentbox</code>, a command that a frontend runs can read the frontend&#39;s own credentials.</strong> The <code>srt</code> settings of a frontend allow reads of its own credential files by name, and every other frontend&#39;s settings deny them: <code>~/.claude.json</code>, <code>~/.claude/.credentials.json</code> and the Anthropic profile&#39;s <code>~/.config/anthropic/credentials</code> for <code>claude</code>; <code>auth.json</code> and <code>mcp-auth.json</code> in <code>~/.local/share/opencode</code> for <code>opencode</code>; <code>agent.db</code>, <code>agent.db-wal</code> and <code>.env</code> in <code>~/.omp/agent</code> for <code>omp</code>; and <code>~/.gemini/jetski-standalone-oauth-token</code> for <code>antigravity</code>. The sandbox of the whole process cannot tell the frontend from its own shell. The <code>claude</code> settings also allow writes to <code>~/.config/anthropic/credentials</code> and to the lock directory <code>~/.config/anthropic/credentials.lock</code>, which Claude Code makes before a token refresh.</li><li><strong>An allowed host takes what a command sends to it.</strong> The network allowlist names hosts, not methods, so a sandboxed command can send a write to any host of <code>allowedDomains</code>. The harness list holds no <code>archive.org</code>, because its <code>*.archive.org</code> form would cover <code>web.archive.org</code>, whose Save Page Now publishes any URL without a login, and the upload host <code>s3.us.archive.org</code>. Of the hosts below <code>archive.org</code>, it holds <code>scholar.archive.org</code> alone. A host that accepts writes and that a session only reads is in the list <code>fetch</code>: the <code>WebFetch</code> tool reaches it with a GET, and a sandboxed command does not reach it. A GET can still carry data in its URL to such a host. Under <code>agentbox</code> a frontend does not reach a <code>fetch</code> host at all, because srt takes <code>allowedDomains</code> alone.</li><li><strong>An MCP server runs outside the session&#39;s sandbox; on macOS, Kaimon runs under a profile of its own.</strong> The sandbox wraps the shell commands of a session. An MCP server is a process of its own, for example a server that launchd starts, and the code that it runs for a session does not see the session&#39;s write allowlist, read deny list or network allowlist. The permission lists control which of its tools a session can call. The launch agents of Kaimon and <code>julia-update</code> start their programs under a Seatbelt profile, which denies every file read and write that it does not name. Kaimon&#39;s profile leaves open the network and every write inside <code>~/Research</code> outside the repository parts that it denies, such as <code>~/Research/.claude/hooks</code>, which the session sandbox denies: through Kaimon&#39;s <code>ex</code>, code can write a hook that a later session runs outside every sandbox. A process under Kaimon&#39;s profile can also write text into any terminal of the user. <a href="./sandbox#the-daemon-profiles">The daemon profiles</a> give the rules in full. On Linux, and for every other MCP server, no profile applies.</li><li><strong>OpenCode and oh-my-pi have no sandbox of their own.</strong> Started without <code>agentbox</code>, their permission blocks and guard extensions check text, as the deny list and the hooks do. Started through <a href="./sandbox#the-launcher-agentbox"><code>agentbox</code></a>, each runs with every command that it starts inside the sandbox of <code>srt</code>. Antigravity runs commands in its terminal sandbox in the CLI and the desktop app, and its guard hook sets <code>BypassSandbox</code> on each command and checks paths and command segments.</li><li><strong>A session cannot edit the settings.</strong> The template denies an edit of <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code> and of <code>~/.claude.json</code>. A change of the settings is a change that you install yourself.</li></ul><h2 id="check-a-change-of-the-settings" tabindex="-1">Check a change of the settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#check-a-change-of-the-settings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Check a change of the settings&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Before <code>harness install --apply</code>, read the diff of the sections <code>permissions</code>, <code>hooks</code>, <code>sandbox</code> and <code>modelSettings</code> that the dry run prints after the line of <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>. After the <code>--apply</code>, run <code>harness install</code> again. It must print <code>already identical</code> for <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code> and exit 0.</p><p>Then check the change in a new session. For each changed rule, run more than one test: a call that the rule must stop, and a call that it must let through. One result can mislead, and a result that lets a call through is the more dangerous one. Repeat the exact call before you decide that a rule does not work.</p><h2 id="planned-safeguards" tabindex="-1">Planned safeguards <a class="header-anchor" href="#planned-safeguards" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Planned safeguards&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These safeguards are decided, and none of them runs yet. <a href="./sandbox#planned">Sandboxes</a> gives each sandbox item in more detail.</p><ul><li><strong>A guard against a session with no sandbox</strong> (planned): a guard hook refuses every tool call when <code>~/.ssh</code> is readable and no sandbox is on.</li><li><strong>The daemon profiles on Linux</strong> (work in progress): the Kaimon server and the <code>julia-update</code> job under <code>bwrap</code>, with the rules of the macOS profiles.</li><li><strong>Kaimon without the network</strong> (planned): only the package servers stay reachable.</li><li><strong>The desktop apps under an outer sandbox</strong> (planned), and <strong>a Linux launcher</strong> (planned).</li><li><strong>Masked keys</strong> (planned), and <strong>a forge token per owner</strong> (planned).</li><li><strong>A credential proxy</strong> (planned, optional and last).</li></ul>',12))])}const x=i(l,[["render",m]]);export{k as __pageData,x as default};
